{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"sour-plugugly-rhino","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe `rebar-root-disk-pressure` CloudWatch alarm (root `/`, 30G, threshold 85%) sat in ALARM for ~39 hours on 2026-08-04/06: the review-bot host's root disk filled from ~48% to ~95% during an autodeploy build burst, then stayed pinned at 92-96% until the next real deploy finally reclaimed it. Root cause: `infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh` reclaims docker garbage via `prune_docker_caches()` (builder prune `--keep-storage 5GB` + image prune) ONLY on the deploy and backoff paths. When `main` is quiescent, every ~2-min `rebar-autodeploy.timer` tick hits the \"up to date … no-op\" early exit (autodeploy.sh:228) and returns WITHOUT pruning, so nothing reclaims disk between deploys. This extends closed bug 7a41-95f4-c38c-4b5a (added the deploy-path prune) to cover the quiescent-main gap it left.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd a pressure-triggered, throttled reclaim to the autodeploy no-op path — the ideal hook because it fires every ~2 min exactly when no deploy is reclaiming, already holds the single-flight `flock` (autodeploy.sh:191), and already has `prune_docker_caches()` in scope. Reuse the exact root-disk-% computation from observability.sh:79 (`df --output=pcent /`).\n\n1. Add tunables near `BUILD_CACHE_KEEP` (autodeploy.sh:~87): `DISK_PRESSURE_PCT` (default 80 — below the 85% alarm for hysteresis), `PRESSURE_PRUNE_MIN_INTERVAL` (default 600s throttle), `PRESSURE_PRUNE_TS_FILE` (default under `STATE_DIR`).\n2. Add two helpers near the existing helpers (autodeploy.sh:~150): `root_disk_pct()` (the df one-liner) and `reclaim_under_pressure()` which returns early unless `root_disk_pct >= DISK_PRESSURE_PCT`, enforces the min-interval throttle via the persisted timestamp file (atomic tmp+mv write), emits a countable `AUTODEPLOY_DISK_PRESSURE` journal marker via the existing `marker()`, calls `prune_docker_caches`, and logs the before/after percentage. A prune failure only logs (never alters control flow), matching the deploy-path guarantees.\n3. Call `reclaim_under_pressure` on the no-op early-exit line (autodeploy.sh:228), before the `log \"up to date\"; exit 0`.\n\nDefault threshold 80% with a flat floor plus the 10-min throttle (not a derivative/trend) is chosen deliberately: the throttle prevents per-tick churn and `prune_docker_caches` keeps a 5GB warm cache so back-to-back prunes are cheap no-ops; an absolute floor is simpler and less flappy than slope tracking. All new behavior is opt-outable via env overrides sourced from `/etc/rebar/autodeploy.env` (already sourced by the script), so no host redeploy is required to tune it.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh` — add the three tunables, the `root_disk_pct()` + `reclaim_under_pressure()` helpers, and the single call site on the no-op exit path. No change to the deploy/backoff prune paths, the drain/defer logic, or any journal-marker token except the new `AUTODEPLOY_DISK_PRESSURE`.\n- `tests/scripts/test_autodeploy_disk_pressure.py` — new test module (new testable behavior): assert the reclaim fires when simulated disk% is at/above the threshold and no-ops below it, that the throttle suppresses a second prune inside the interval, and that a prune failure does not change the script's exit code. Stub `df` and `docker` via PATH shims / injected fakes, matching the existing `tests/scripts/test_autodeploy_*` harness style.\n\nThe new `AUTODEPLOY_DISK_PRESSURE` marker is intentionally NOT wired to a new CloudWatch metric in this story (autodeploy still exits 0; the existing `root_disk_used_percent` alarm already covers the outcome). Wiring an observability delta metric for it is deferred as out of scope.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Happy path: with a `df` shim reporting 90% and a `docker` shim that records calls, running the no-op path invokes `prune_docker_caches` once and writes the timestamp file.\n- Throttle/edge path: a second invocation within `PRESSURE_PRUNE_MIN_INTERVAL` does NOT invoke prune; an invocation with `df` reporting 70% (below threshold) does NOT invoke prune.\n- Failure path: a `docker` shim that exits non-zero leaves the script's overall exit status at 0 (prune failure is non-fatal), and the pressure marker is still emitted.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] On the autodeploy no-op tick, when root `/` usage is >= `DISK_PRESSURE_PCT` (default 80) and the throttle window has elapsed, `prune_docker_caches` runs — verified by `tests/scripts/test_autodeploy_disk_pressure.py` happy-path assertion (docker-shim call recorded).\n- [ ] When root usage is below the threshold, or within the throttle window of the last pressure-prune, no prune runs — verified by the below-threshold and throttle assertions in the same test module.\n- [ ] A prune failure on the pressure path does not change autodeploy's exit code (stays 0) and only logs — verified by the failure-path assertion.\n- [ ] A countable `AUTODEPLOY_DISK_PRESSURE` marker is emitted to stderr when the pressure prune triggers — asserted in the test module.\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass; the new and existing `tests/scripts/test_autodeploy_*` tests pass under the repo venv.","id":"28f9-535c-ce9c-4583","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["infra","observability","autodeploy"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"autodeploy: pressure-triggered docker prune on the no-op tick to prevent root-disk-pressure"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786133524467032001,"uuid":"bc3c09b4-2751-43f7-9484-ee7e3fc24de7"}